From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
Subject: [PATCH] TEST: wifi: ath11k: experiments on scatter-gather extension descriptor frames

Add module parameters, 0644 and off by default, read for each frame,
that change how a frame goes out through an MSDU extension descriptor.
They apply, with tso set, to plain IPv4 or IPv6 TCP frames to a station;
with several set the first in this list wins, and a frame the chosen one
cannot take is sent the normal way:

ext_sg_swcsum: a frame with fragments gets its CHECKSUM_PARTIAL resolved
by skb_checksum_help() and goes out with no checksum enable in the TCL
data command; the vendor enables the L3 and L4 checksums only for a
frame that asks for checksum offload or is TSO (wifi-host-cmn
dp/wifi3.0/li/dp_li_tx.c:577-582).

ext_sg_as_tso: a CHECKSUM_PARTIAL frame with fragments goes out as a TSO
batch of one segment: the headers in buffer 0, the rest of the linear
part and the fragments after them, tso_enable 1, the TCP flags of the
header under the mask 0x1ff, the L2 length, the IP length, the TCP
sequence number and the IPv4 ID, and the checksum enables of a batch.
The TCP checksum field keeps what the stack wrote for CHECKSUM_PARTIAL,
the pseudo-header sum with the length, where a GSO batch has it without.

ext_sg_split_da: a frame with fragments goes out in the layout of the
vendor's multicast enhancement (wifi-host-cmn dp/wifi3.0/dp_txrx_me.c:
562-615): buffer 0 the 6 byte destination address, copied to a buffer of
its own (one L1_CACHE_BYTES buffer for each descriptor, allocated with
the descriptors) and mapped by itself, buffer 1 the rest of the linear
part, then the fragments, so at most four of them; checksum in software,
no enables. The address buffer is unmapped with the fragments wherever
the descriptor is released: completion, the crash flush and the cleanup.

ext_one_buf: a linear frame goes out through an extension descriptor
with one buffer, the whole frame; checksum in software, no enables.

ext_log_first is now the number of the next extension descriptor frames
to log, of any station; writing it arms that many again (default 8). A
logged frame gets, per segment, the existing map line and a line with
the 18 words of its descriptor as written, then a line with the 8 words
of the TCL ring entry of its first segment (TLV header and command) as
written, and the frame line, which names the layout; a logged frame that
falls back gets a line with the reason.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
